SM(474,{"n":"Acts 18","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Acts 18.mp3","d":3151.0,"s":[[0.4,"Come and study Holy Scripture with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.48,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed tradition, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.36,"Go ahead and get started."],[22.24,"And when we get started in uh just a moment, we'll be picking up at uh the beginning of uh Acts of the Apostles chapter 18."],[32.16,"As I always say, uh by the time anyone is hearing this, and as we speak, we're currently 43 episodes ahead of what's being released on the internet, so it will be a while."],[47.92,"But uh once somebody is uh is hearing this, uh, the first Bible study of the Acts of the Apostles will also be available online uh to listen to to hear my introduction. to the book and some of the themes of the book"],[62.76,"But for now, uh to kind of get us caught up uh to where we are and where we're picking up tonight."],[69.96,"Uh we're in the midst of uh St."],[72.52,"Paul's second missionary journey."],[74.76,"Uh St. Paul has taken St."],[77.16,"Silas and St."],[78.28,"Timothy with him, and uh they began by revisiting churches that St."],[83.64,"Paul had planted with St."],[85.24,"Barnabas in Asia Minor, which is now Turkey."],[89.8,"Uh, and then uh after a dream, uh St."],[93.72,"Paul was directed to uh go into Macedonia, and from Macedonia he has now gone down into Greece proper."],[101.32,"Uh, when we finished up the last chapter, uh, we had read about St."],[105.36,"Paul's visit to Athens, which is Luke is with them now, too."],[109.76,"Yes, and Saint Luke is is with them now too."],[112.32,"Uh we commented on that because there was this switch from they, they, they to we."],[119.14,"Because Saint Luke the author is now also uh accompanying them in this section of the book."],[125.94,"Uh and uh as we mentioned last time, Athens is as Greece as you can get."],[131.86,"So they are now fully in Greece."],[134.82,"And uh we saw that especially in Athens, uh St."],[139.46,"Paul's normal strategy in all of these cities has been to first go to the synagogue uh and to take the opportunity to preach there in the synagogue about Christ, uh, both to the members of the Jewish community, obviously, who are in the synagogue, and then sort of the surrounding layer of interested Gentiles who frequented the synagogue and frequently also endowed the synagogue uh but who were not going to go all the way to become actually become Jews themselves."],[171.82,"And we've seen Saint Paul getting a significant number of commers of converts, particularly from that second group of those interested Gentiles."],[180.86,"And so that has caused a lot of strife between Saint Paul and these Jewish communities, because the members of the Jewish community who don't accept Jesus as the Messiah aren't happy about St."],[193.66,"Paul's sort of raiding their not only their members but also their donors and their supporters in the city and drawing them off, you know, to become Christians."],[209.38,"And so we've seen this strife over and over, but we also saw that particularly when St."],[213.22,"Paul came to Athens, he also branched out beyond that because of sort of the particular culture of Athens, he had the opportunity not only to preach in the synagogues, which he did, but also to go out the other days of the week besides the Sabbath out into the marketplace."],[231.78,"Well, below the Parthenon, yeah."],[233.7,"Yeah. But to go out into the marketplace and where there were a lot of people hanging around to to discuss the news of the day and discuss ideas and philosophy and politics and everything else going on."],[246.3,"And that made a ready audience for him to approach the people."],[249.58,"So we saw him for the first time really in the book of Acts directing a sermon to just straight out pagans, you know, with sort of no direct connection to the Old Testament scriptures or the Jewish community or any of that, but speaking about Christ to them."],[268.94,"And we saw how to do that, he sort of went way back into the scriptures, back to the Tower of Fabel, back to you know, the point before the nations were separated and before Abraham was called to begin Israel."],[283.34,"He went back to, okay, I have to go way back, but there is a point in our heritage that we have in common, and that's that God created all of us, you know, and that what's his and then Tower of Babel happened, and since then you've been following these other things that aren't really gods, but the true God who created you and created everything is now calling you back."],[304.58,"Calling you back to himself."],[306.34,"It just made me think there's no letter of Paul to the Athenians."],[310.5,"There's nothing. It would be really interesting, uh, because it would have that different angle to it."],[317.94,"Yeah, probably the closest thing we have to that, although it's debated, but probably the closest thing we have to that is the the letter to the Colossians."],[330.86,"There's some debate as to whether some of the things in there are addressing paganism or are addressing a particular type of Jewish mysticism."],[337.82,"That's probably a mix of both."],[341.66,"But uh yeah, Colossae uh from what we could tell, Colossians was a city that had a very did not have a strong uh Torah based Jewish background."],[352.94,"In the way that, for example, when he writes his letter to the Galatians, you know, he's talking very much about the law and circumcision and all of these things."],[361.26,"Um Colossians, less so."],[365.02,"It's sort of more uh cosmic issues, you know, of cosmology and these kind of things."],[372.14,"Um but yes, we. we don't have we don't have the uh address to the philosophers that we that might like from St"],[382.02,"Paul directly. Um now we do get that from St."],[385.62,"Justin Martyr in the second century."],[389.3,"That's um he writes uh and addresses sort of the Greek philosophical traditions and how they interact with Christianity, but that's a hundred years later."],[401.94,"Long time away."],[402.98,"Yeah. Okay. Uh so unless there are any other questions, comments, concerns, complaints, uh, we'll go ahead and get started in uh Acts the Apostles chapter 18, verse 1."],[418.98,"So after these things, Paul departed from Athens and went to Corinth"],[427.42,"A couple things about Corinth."],[430.22,"Corinth is one of the more famous cities that St."],[433.42,"Paul visits, because of course he writes 1 and 2 Corinthians to the church at Corinth later."],[441.26,"From what we could tell from the letters and for what St."],[445.1,"Paul says in all of his letters about what letters he had written, we believe he wrote at least four letters to the Corinthians."],[455.1,"And it seems pretty clear that what we call Second Corinthians is actually at least two of those put together."],[462.86,"You could kind of, when you read them in Greek. especially, you could sort of see the seam, you know, where you have two complete letters that have just been put together"],[471.46,"And that would have been done for obvious reasons."],[474.26,"Um lots of biblical books circulated together."],[478.58,"Because especially before you were talking about codices, when you were talking about scrolls, you had a certain amount of space."],[484.18,"And if you're going to pay the money to have it made, you were going to fill the space."],[487.94,"And that even holds true later when you get to codices."],[491.0,"Which looks sort of like our modern books."],[493.48,"A codex, the difference between a codex and a modern book is that a codex was essentially written on a long sheet of paper, sort of like a scroll, but then that was folded up and bound to sort of look like our pages."],[507.4,"And so you were going to want to get your money's worth"],[513.92,"And so we find very, very early, not only do you have a couple of letters put together, but um what we call the minor prophets in the Old Testament, Abus, Obadiah, there are twelve of them."],[527.04,"Those all circulated on one scroll."],[529.2,"It's called the Book of the Twelve."],[531.6,"So especially when you read the Church Fathers and other ancient authors, they refer to all of those as just one book."],[539.84,"Because they're sort of all bound together, because some of them are very short."],[545.04,"The long ones are like four chapters."],[547.52,"And so the same thing happens with St"],[550.88,"Paul's epistles, but by the year 100, all of St."],[553.84,"Paul's epistles have been collected and are circulating together as a collection, not individually."],[560.84,"Same thing happens with what we call the general epistles or the Catholic epistles."],[565.64,"James versus 2 Peter, first second, 3rd John, Jude, for the same reason."],[570.2,"They're quite short."],[571.88,"So they get sort of all put together and copied together as a unit."],[577.96,"So that's most likely why 2 Corinthians is two letters that are now on one head, is they're probably very early since they were both relatively short."],[586.76,"You know. They were written to this by the same person to the same people."],[591.4,"You know, they're just kind of put together into one into one unit and began to circulate that way."],[597.16,"But so uh St."],[598.52,"Paul wrote to Corinth several times."],[599.16,"Not only St"],[602.0,"Paul, though, uh the book that we now call First Clement, which was written at the end of the first century, and you can find that in uh the collection of the Apostolic Fathers, um, was written by uh Saint Clement, who was the uh bishop of Rome uh at the end of the, he was the third after St."],[627.04,"Peter, Saint Linus, and then Saint Clement."],[630.64,"But uh St."],[631.44,"Peter actually mentions Saint Clement in uh 2 Peter."],[636.08,"Um he was an associate, he knew St."],[638.4,"Peter directly. He wrote a letter at the end of the first century to Corinth on on be behalhalf off the of ch theur chchurch. and he says he's writing the whole church at Rome to at Corinth"],[648.76,"Um the reason so many letters get written to Corinth is not that at this time in history Corinth is a really important city."],[658.84,"Uh it was in previous era in a previous era, but not at this point."],[662.84,"It's because there are horrible, horrible problems at the church in Corinth that sort of continue to have toed. awn in this in the state of Mississippi call"],[671.96,"Really severe serious problems."],[679.56,"It's like why they're not going to be able to do that."],[685.6,"Churches are a big mess."],[688.12,"Well, the the original Corinth, uh, because this is actually the second Corinth, this is Roman Corinth that St."],[694.96,"Paul is going to visit and that he's gonna write to."],[697.12,"Greek Corinth, the original Corinth, was an ancient city."],[700.56,"And became a very important city because it's located right at the uh the sort of tiny land bridge between between the northern part of Greece and the Peloponnesus."],[715.64,"And so all the trade roads had to run through it."],[718.92,"But also it became sort of any time the Greek city-states had to interact with each other, it became sort of the locus for doing that."],[728.44,"Such that the defense league, sort of the defense pact, sort of the NATO of Greece."],[733.86,"That all the Greek city states made for mutual protection was called the Corinthian League."],[739.3,"Huh. So because that's where they all met."],[742.9,"It was the central location."],[745.14,"Um so because of the trade and because of that political importance, it became very wealthy."],[750.58,"Because it became very wealthy, it became very decadent."],[754.79,"And so uh Corinthianized was used as a verb in Greek that meant that someone was really debauched."],[762.23,"Wow. Like the way we say party like a rock star."],[765.31,"They would say party like a Corinthian."],[767.27,"That was so that was sort of the wild new Orleans, the wild town."],[774.45,"The wild town."],[775.85,"Um now when uh when Rome invaded Greece uh in the middle of the second century BC, uh Corinth, because of the Corinthian League, was sort of setting itself up once again to become and because of the isthmus, to become sort of a defensive point for Greece on the Peloponnesus below."],[798.61,"And so Rome made an example out of Corinth and burned it to the ground."],[804.37,"So it was completely leveled."],[807.41,"Um to sort of, and then they moved on to the Peloponnesus, and they sort of all looked at Corinth and said, Oh, well, hey, maybe we don't need to resist."],[814.85,"Maybe this is a good thing."],[818.89,"And so then the new Corinth was built by the Romans on as a Roman city on top of the ruins of the original Corinth."],[830.01,"So it was rebuilt as a display of sort of Roman power to the Greeks."],[836.41,"And so that is the city that St."],[839.45,"Paul is now coming to."],[841.61,"That said, Roman Corinth did develop a similar reputation."],[847.93,"It was also wealthy in a trade city for the same reason, but it also developed a somewhat similar reputation."],[853.77,"And so some of the things we see him attacking in in the letter to the Corinthians, like apparently a man had been having an affair with his own mother-in-law."],[867.09,"Uh yeah. So are the kind of things that you would expect to happen in Corinth, but that's why we see St."],[875.25,"Paul again and again saying, Okay, now that you guys are in Christ, we need to be different than the other people in Corinth."],[884.45,"We've left all that behind us now."],[886.95,"That way of living."],[888.55,"We need to stop now."],[889.75,"But not it wasn't just a sort of moral issue, there was there was also a major pagan religious center."],[897.35,"Um we have uh it was the center of of a mystery cult uh dedicated to Demeter, who is the god of the goddess of the harvest, and uh the huge temple. there has been excavated very thoroughly"],[912.99,"Is it the Elicinian mysteries?"],[915.23,"Is it no?"],[915.95,"This is a particular Corinthian."],[918.59,"It probably evolved from a more traditional Demeter temple, but it became sort of this initiation initiatory cult."],[928.43,"But uh one of the interesting things about it is that a good chunk of the temple complex are these huge banquet halls for meals."],[939.63,"And in the excavations of these banquet halls, they found the remains of all kinds of animals."],[946.39,"Mostly pigs, because pig pigs were the primary animals sacrificed by Greeks to their gods."],[953.75,"That was the primary sacrificial animal"],[957.95,"And so we forget, and we'll talk about this more when we get into 1 Corinthians, because St."],[964.27,"Paul's going to make a point of this, but we forget that sacrifices were meals."],[969.87,"A lot of our Protestant friends, when they talk about the Eucharist, want to make a distinction."],[974.75,"They say, oh, the Eucharist isn't a sacrifice, it's a meal."],[977.87,"In the ancient world, that distinction didn't make sense."],[980.91,"Because sacrifices were meals."],[983.63,"You went, you sacrificed the animal to the gods."],[987.41,"And you offered a portion of it to the gods, and then the rest of it, the people who were offering the sacrifice, gathered and ate in this celebratory."],[996.05,"And they were considered themselves to be eating a meal with. right part since part of it went to the other, they're eating a meal with the gods"],[1001.93,"This binds the community together, binds the community together with its god."],[1007.15,"And so was had social and political elements in addition to the religious element."],[1013.55,"And this is this is uh this makes clear sort of what St."],[1017.63,"Paul was getting at when he spent so much time in first and second Corinthians, talking about meat offered to idols, and talking about you know what's someone gonna think if they see you eating in a pagan temple, because this was a major part of the civic life of Corinth, was participating in these meals."],[1035.47,"And in addition to the pig bones, they found everything: the sea urchin remains, like all kinds of things, and they're not sure if they were a sacrificing those other things too, or if it was just they were sacrificing the pigs and then having other stuff on the table as part of the feast."],[1054.07,"I eat sea sea urchins in Sicily."],[1058.31,"It was a Roman thing, yeah."],[1060.71,"Yeah. Um but yes, this was this this major thing."],[1066.79,"Uh another note on their sacrifices."],[1069.43,"A lot of the Greeks who were sort of impious used to joke about the sacrifices and refer to it as the burning of the bones, because all the useful parts of the animal they keep."],[1080.95,"They just burnt the parts for the god that they couldn't do anything with, like the bones. teeth you know you know and hooves and that kind of thing"],[1092.99,"Yeah burn that you know but the meat and the leather and the all that stuff they they kept for themselves and offered the rest."],[1100.27,"And that that's important when we loop around eventually when we get to the end of the New Testament get back into the old testament when God is gives those very particular instructions about what parts they're to offer to God you'll notice they're all the best parts."],[1116.68,"So that's very different than the surrounding you know cultures."],[1120.84,"God says, No, I want the best parts."],[1122.64,"You take for yourselves the you know the other parts and do whatever you want with it."],[1130.76,"Well that's all through Leviticus and the instructions where he's saying, you know, this piece, this piece goes to the priests, this piece goes to the people."],[1137.92,"These are the pieces that are burnt."],[1139.28,"The pieces that are burnt are all the sort of choice."],[1143.33,"Prize portions. But the the pagans did the exact opposite."],[1147.73,"They went and uh burned the uh the useless parts."],[1152.37,"Um but so that this is the uh I'm going through all this to give you an idea of sort of the civic life of Corinth and what that was like, and that uh it was the the idolatry and the pagan religion and sort of the the uh liberal sexuality, shall we say, of the of the culture were all very much on display in public that's what it meant to be a Corinthian and so that's why trying to have a Corinthian church made up of converted Corinthian people is gonna require it's gonna be a high maintenance church for St."],[1192.82,"Paul where he's gonna have to you know sort of continuously address, continuously remind, continuously you know uh I've heard of of something called AcroCorinth."],[1205.14,"Is that like the Acropolis of Corinth or something."],[1208.06,"Yeah, that's the that's and that's where a lot of those excavations have been of the temple complexes and stuff."],[1214.98,"Yeah, the uh Greek cities and Roman cities had what's called an Acropolis."],[1219.3,"Acro is the same place where you get like acrobat, it means high."],[1223.34,"High up. So there would be there would be um a hill or a mound, depending on the city, how big it was, you know, uh up above."],[1232.46,"That's usually where the temples would be and any civic government buildings would be."],[1237.5,"And then you'd have sort of the main part of the city below, and then you'd have the part by the docks, which is where a lot of the commerce and sort of sedier things happened."],[1247.74,"And there was actually sort of a religious understanding of that for Greek and Roman pagans, because the three the three primary Olympian gods were uh Zeus and uh and uh Poseidon and Pluto, who were brothers."],[1266.16,"And they considered each of those to have their own sort of realm."],[1271.68,"So Zeus, it sees like the sky, he's the Acropolis, and he's the king of the gods."],[1277.38,"So here's where the power is, and then you had sort of the the run-of-the-mill city and the marketplace and everything, which was Pluto, because Pluto is also money, uh, or Hades."],[1290.42,"Uh, and then you had the docks of the sea, which was Poseidon."],[1295.38,"Um, so they had sort of this it's called by scholars as if they refer to it as cosmic geography."],[1302.36,"Where they had this idea."],[1303.72,"And you see a little bit of that in the Bible, but in the Bible it's mostly related to like Jerusalem, you know, and uh other mountains, an important place, Sinai, uh Tabor, Mount Hermon in Palestine that sort of are used to represent, you know, you read in the Psalms, you know, Zion, the tallest of the mountains, the greatest of the hills."],[1326.48,"It's like, well, not really, but because that's where the temple was."],[1332.66,"So there's this religious understanding to it where religion and geography come together."],[1336.58,"But the pagan version of that had to do with um their cities."],[1341.46,"Now, in actuality, the reason their cities evolved that way was much simpler."],[1345.86,"It was the higher points were the best defended, you know, if you were attacked."],[1358.22,"It was a philosophical argumentative strategy where it's you present a strategy, they retract from it, and you retort to the I'll I'll pay you that."],[1368.06,"But it's it's the same principle."],[1369.58,"You fall back to the higher around."],[1372.54,"Yeah. Um but so this is the civic."],[1375.26,"So these are the kind of cities now that St."],[1377.02,"Paul is going to to bring the gospel."],[1379.82,"This is sort of the thick of pagan Roman life."],[1384.62,"Yeah. Yeah. He's not sort of visiting these little towns not far from Palestine and visiting the synagogue anymore and talking to some of his Jewish countrymen."],[1394.86,"He's now right out there in the and we're gonna see that's sometimes going to cause some issues."],[1404.42,"Okay, so he's now, after leaving Athens, uh St."],[1408.18,"Paul comes to Corinth."],[1413.54,"And he found a certain Jew named Aquila, born in Pontus, who had recently come from Italy with his wife Priscilla, because Claudius had commanded all the Jews to depart from Rome and he came to them."],[1429.38,"This is actually one, of several in the first century times when all the Jews were expelled from Rome."],[1437.7,"Claudius did it uh for the same reason it was done most of the time."],[1443.46,"Uh, before they started using Christians for this purpose, which came later, Jews were the scapegoat when things went wrong for the Roman Empire."],[1455.02,"So Rome would suffer some defeat or catastrophe."],[1458.22,"They would say, well, why is this?"],[1460.46,"We talked a little bit about this last time, how the uh the Romans believe they had sort of a quid pro quo with the gods."],[1469.26,"That you know, we do the ritual, we do it the right way, we do it what we're supposed to, and then the gods are supposed to hold up their end and give us success in war or whatever it is."],[1480.3,"And uh and they they uh had this set of books called the Sibyline Oracles, which were later destroyed by the Christian governor. in the fifth century, but um that were these ancient books that were supposedly written cryptically"],[1496.82,"We don't have them."],[1498.5,"And we only have the quotes we have from them are even kind of dubious."],[1502.82,"Uh because the only people they were stored under one of the temples in Rome, and the only people who are allowed to look at them were a couple of priests at any given time."],[1511.14,"But they had all these cryptic sort of Nostradamus-like prophecies that was supposed to be about the whole history of Rome."],[1518.34,"And so anytime they were facing some issue."],[1522.31,"They'd go and they'd send the priest to consult the books."],[1525.57,"And they would come out and say, Well, the books say we need to whatever, offer these sacrifices, you know, go and do this. retreat, don't go into battle because we'll lose"],[1537.01,"Whatever it was."],[1538.23,"For their interpretation of the books."],[1541.75,"And a lot of times these would be things even things that we would consider small, like there's a comet."],[1552.29,"Oh no, there's a comet."],[1553.67,"What do we do?"],[1554.48,"Go get right, go consult the books."],[1556.32,"But it could also include things like when Hannibal invaded Italy."],[1561.26,"Uh they consulted the books, and the books told them to go and find uh one Roman and one, I forget what kind of barbarian it was."],[1570.3,"I think maybe a Scythian."],[1572.7,"And uh bury them alive in the marketplace in Rome."],[1576.46,"So they went and did it."],[1576.86,"That's how seriously they took it"],[1578.68,"That's how seriously they took it."],[1583.08,"Whatever it told them to do, they would do."],[1584.76,"But then, see, the quid pro quo is okay, we went and did what the book said, so now we're gonna beat Hannibal."],[1590.28,"Uh-huh. Now, there are by the Middle Ages, there are Christians who are interpreting these books as prophesying the coming of Christ."],[1600.14,"But they're not the actual books."],[1602.14,"Uh-huh. So what happens is in the fifth century when Rome is falling and getting sacked."],[1608.74,"There was a big group of people in Rome who said, This is because we turned Christian."],[1613.74,"Yeah. We need to go back and consult the books and find out what to do to get the pagan gods back on our side."],[1620.22,"And the Christian. governor was outnumbered and everything"],[1624.42,"And so his response to that was to go and bring the books out and publicly burn them."],[1629.94,"Say, nope, there's no going back."],[1634.18,"Now, after that, when you get into the sixth and seventh centuries, there were sort of counterfeit ones that were made."],[1643.06,"And a lot of them sort of because they were being made by early medieval Christians, worked at a lot of stuff, and then they could point to it and say, See, you know, Christ's coming was prophesied by the ancient Roman prophets, too."],[1656.26,"But those are all now known to be bogus."],[1659.06,"But in the Middle Ages, a lot you know, they were considered authentic in various places."],[1662.5,"But so that just to give this idea of the quid pro quo idea"],[1667.98,"So if they did what they were supposed to do and then things didn't go their way."],[1672.88,"We well, now we have a religious crisis here."],[1677.12,"And one of the very easy ways to resolve that crisis, once Rome had a big Jewish community, was well, wait, we all did the sacrifices."],[1688.32,"We all went and worshiped the gods."],[1690.08,"But these people didn't."],[1693.84,"These people refused to sacrifice to our gods."],[1697.12,"That must be, they must have gotten mad that these people didn't do it."],[1701.68,"And that's why things didn't work out the way they were supposed to."],[1705.9,"And so they became sort of a scapegoat, the Jewish community in Rome."],[1710.26,"And so this happened under Claudius, and Claudius expelled them all from Rome."],[1714.76,"They couldn't come into the city of Rome."],[1716.28,"That way, when Rome, with the capital, when they went to go worship the gods, it was everybody."],[1723.26,"And so uh that, of course, would later Nero in particular."],[1729.24,"Would was Nero was the first emperor to really do it, would uh use the exact same reasoning to blame the Christians."],[1738.09,"It's these Christians and their bizarre practices, and they won't sacrifice to our gods, and they won't do this, so it's their fault."],[1743.69,"So that's where a lot of the persecution of Christians is going to come from later on."],[1749.37,"But so this has already happened now under Claudius, and because of that, all these Jews who are thrown out of Rome have had to come and relocate, find someplace else to live."],[1761.54,"And uh so we have this couple, um Aquila and Priscilla."],[1767.7,"Now, later when St."],[1768.98,"Paul talks about them, uh in 1st and 2 Corinthians, for example, he lists her first."],[1776.58,"He lists them as Priscilla and Aquila."],[1779.7,"Yeah. He lists the Priscilla and Aquila, he lists the wife first."],[1783.94,"And so it's a pretty sure bet that she was probably the one from the family with the money."],[1792.34,"Because for Jews to live in the capital in Rome, they would have. have had to some money and or connections, you know, to be able to live there"],[1804.22,"And so when they and then when they leave, they move to Corinth, which is this big bustling central city."],[1810.46,"But the fact that so they have fairly certainly had money, but the fact that she tends to get named first implies that it was probably through her family that they had money rather than through his."],[1823.74,"And they both have Latin names."],[1825.9,"Yes. Yeah, Priscilla and Aquila are both."],[1828.94,"They've been in Italy a long time."],[1830.14,"Latin names, yeah."],[1831.5,"Yeah. Um. And are acculturated."],[1835.34,"You know, that which is also a reason why, you know, you think, well, if they're expelled from Rome, why didn't they go to Jerusalem?"],[1840.62,"Why didn't they go back to where the temple was?"],[1842.74,"Well, they probably didn't know a lick of Aramaic or they were probably firmly acculturated in Greco-Roman culture."],[1850.5,"And so you get right out of Rome, the center of the empire, you go to another major cultural center, which is you know Corinth, Alexandria, you know, Athens."],[1865.3,"Okay. So St."],[1867.62,"Paul finds them there."],[1871.06,"So because he was of the same trade, he stayed with them and worked."],[1879.38,"Sorry. Work worked for by occupation, they do."],[1885.22,"This doesn't sound like a very important business to us"],[1891.34,"We don't use a lot of tents anymore."],[1894.38,"Camping circuses. The occasional rental for an outdoor party."],[1898.48,"That's about that's about it for us."],[1902.0,"But when you consider the Roman military, which if they were doing this in Rome was probably their primary customer."],[1909.98,"Always need tent needs tents when they're on maneuvers when they're traveling."],[1913.82,"But just travelers in general."],[1915.66,"If you're gonna go any great distance doing any trade, once you get past a certain point, especially if you're taking the roads east, you know, we for we forget that there was a very vital cultural and and uh uh economic trade going on with India, with uh you know what's now Iran, Afghanistan, with the east from the Roman Empire."],[1943.0,"And there there weren't always cities, you know."],[1945.8,"Once you get past a certain point in the Roman Empire, you're gonna be doing some camping, some caravanning."],[1951.89,"And so this this was an important industry."],[1956.21,"And would have included not just the tanning and everything involved in leather and canvas and these sort of things, but also the support structures and you know, all of those things."],[1967.57,"And so this apparently was uh a trade that St."],[1972.77,"Paul knew, that he had worked in, uh, which was not abnormal at that time."],[1979.75,"Um, it wasn't like it is now."],[1982.55,"We we we talk all the time about how St."],[1984.31,"Paul had rabbinical training, but it's not like he went to seminary and had a student loan, you know, paid his expenses and right, did these things."],[1993.69,"So he he would have he would have you've been working some kind of trade, and then as we talked about when we talked about St."],[2002.81,"Paul going and speaking in the synagogues, it's not like even these rabbis, most of them, that was you know, being a rabbi was a full-time job, you know."],[2011.65,"You know, uh, many of them would work, were working some trade during the week."],[2016.65,"This is very common."],[2017.69,"And we're gonna see this reflected in St."],[2018.81,"Paul's gonna put a big emphasis on people working with their hands"],[2020.29,"Paul's epistles. St."],[2026.45,"And uh this is gonna play out in the Father."],[2028.69,"St. John Chrysostom especially lights on it."],[2031.09,"St. John Chrysostom is very suspicious of wealthy people who became wealthy without, you know, working or making things themselves."],[2041.67,"People who sort of became wealthy by shifting money around, you know, and making deals and these kind of things."],[2049.43,"You know, he's gonna look on that a little shamely, but that comes from St."],[2052.95,"Paul. St. Paul has this big emphasis of, you know, honest work, doing good honest work, you know, and uh supporting yourself."],[2061.75,"Something aside from usury."],[2063.27,"Yeah Um and so he encounters uh Priscilla Aquila, meets him there, they have a tent making operation, which they've sort of taken with them now to Corinth."],[2079.55,"Um and so since this happens to be the trade that St."],[2083.31,"Paul knows, he works with them to sort of support himself and support support his mission."],[2089.55,"Uh he's gonna make a point of that as well in his uh uh in his epistles that St."],[2096.19,"Paul never wanted to, you know, take money for the in exchange for the gospel."],[2101.97,"He didn't want to sort of show up in town to preach and then be like, so who's gonna put me up and feed me?"],[2107.69,"You know, kind of thing."],[2109.37,"Because he didn't he didn't even want to give the appearance that he was sort of in this for the money, you know, and he was you know trying to run a racket."],[2117.61,"He wanted to be sort of abrupt reproach in that area."],[2120.01,"And so he could do that."],[2121.21,"He he did take collections, but he didn't take those collections and keep them."],[2124.81,"When he took collections, he was taking collections primarily for the church in Jerusalem because it was mostly Palestinian Jewish peasants who were very poor."],[2134.73,"And so he would ask them to contribute, but we're gonna see he takes a very large collection that he's taken from these new Gentile churches that are more wealthy, and he takes it back to give it to these poorer Christians in these other churches."],[2147.29,"He doesn't sort of use it to buy himself a private jet or a big house or some other things that we occasionally see clergymen in America do."],[2161.41,"This is not Jim and Tammy Faye."],[2163.41,"Yes, this is the opposite of that sort of that sort of idea."],[2170.93,"And he reasoned in the synagogue every Sabbath and persuaded both Jews and Greeks."],[2176.61,"So this is what he does when he first comes to Corinth."],[2179.33,"He says that he's met these people, he has this opportunity to make some money through work to support himself and the mission."],[2187.25,"And so he's doing that six days, and then on the Sabbath, he's going and uh and uh preaching in uh the synagogue."],[2196.61,"Um then on the on the Lord's Day, um they're having the Christian meeting with the Eucharist, and I bring this up because I'm going to digress for a moment because this is one of my pet peeves in terms of uh very often misquoted Bible verses."],[2216.09,"And and this is in one of St."],[2217.77,"Paul's letters to the Corinthians, so it pertains right here."],[2221.53,"That is his sentence: he who does not work shall not eat."],[2228.09,"Now you may think, even based on what I just said, that this is part of St."],[2231.85,"Paul's emphasis on working with your hands."],[2235.33,"But if you take it in context, St."],[2237.97,"Paul is talking about the Eucharist, and what he says. is that certain people are showing up very early on the Lord's Day"],[2250.09,"And they're getting to, at this time, remember, we talked about how Christians are still going to the synagogue on the Sabbath."],[2258.51,"And hearing the scriptures and preaching and that kind of thing, sort of the first part of our liturgy."],[2263.23,"And then meeting on Sunday for the Eucharist for the second part of our liturgy."],[2268.19,"And the Eucharist was being celebrated within what was called an agape feast or an agape meal, a love feast or a love meal, which was meaning there was a full meal in the same way that Christ originally ate a meal with his disciples, and then at the end of it, the Eucharist, it was a similar kind of thing."],[2286.35,"So there was more food there than just the Eucharist."],[2289.51,"Um but what was happening is that people were coming, were showing up early and eating all the food."],[2298.45,"So that the people who came later, there was nothing left for them."],[2303.49,"Okay. And the problem St."],[2306.05,"Paul has with this is that the people who are showing up early are showing up early because they don't work."],[2320.45,"That's how we're wealthy."],[2322.69,"It's because they're wealthy and they don't have to."],[2326.91,"They're wealthy, so they don't have to work on Sunday."],[2330.11,"They don't have to work on the first day of the week."],[2332.55,"They can get up, go over to the church, and start eating."],[2336.01,"The people who are showing up later are the people who had to go work."],[2340.57,"And they go and put in a day's work and then come to the gathering afterwards, and find nothing left for them."],[2351.38,"So that verse actually means the exact opposite of the way it's usually interpreted."],[2357.54,"I had an argument with the Episcopal Bishop of New Orleans, who wrote to the paper in opposition to welfare, to government welfare programs."],[2371.06,"And he quoted that verse."],[2374.1,"And uh liberal me, I was very offended"],[2374.34,"And I wrote him a letter and said I thought this went against all kinds of things and Christian teaching."],[2386.78,"And um he wrote back and more or less politely brushed me away."],[2394.22,"Yeah, it's the opposite."],[2395.5,"And see, part again, it's like I mentioned with St."],[2398.78,"John Chrysostom, part of St."],[2400.22,"Paul's emphasis is right, there are people in the Roman Empire, like Priscilla and Aquila, who are doing well, but they've doing well because they've done well at a trade."],[2412.78,"They've they've worked."],[2414.96,"And so they're so they're doing well."],[2416.64,"There are also people who've just sort of inherited money and live lives of leisure, you know, and who aren't working."],[2419.2,"And St Paul's answer to them is that they need to give up their wealth and live by the work of their hands."],[2433.0,"And this is where that emphasis is going to come into monasticism."],[2437.66,"Where we see like St."],[2438.7,"Anthony the Great, who inherits all this wealth, give it all away."],[2443.76,"And go and live this humble life."],[2446.98,"Supporting himself. This is where that idea comes from in monasticism of monastic poverty."],[2454.14,"It' froms this understanding of, you know, being idly wealthy."],[2459.06,"And it kind of flies in the face of our American culture, because in our American culture, the whole point is I'm going to work for a certain period of time, and then I really want to get to the point where I don't have to anymore, and I can just sort of relax and coast and be comfortable."],[2472.7,"You know? That's and that's not how Christianity works, where our goal is to get to the point where I can just be totally comfortable and indulge myself."],[2483.34,"It's quite the opposite."],[2484.7,"In fact, if I get to that point, I need to start getting rid of some of this stuff until I can't anymore."],[2489.75,"That's asceticism. And and continuing to work on these because it's not good for us to become comfortable and sated and well fed."],[2501.11,"A lot of conservative evangelical denominations have split over um a congregate who makes gets lottery winnings and wants to tithe on it, and there's half that says, oh great, the other half says, No way."],[2516.19,"I wondered about that too."],[2517.95,"Well, you shouldn't be buying uh you shouldn't be gambling."],[2522.67,"So we start there."],[2525.31,"You know, now uh easily come by it's like you you you should be gambling."],[2530.83,"Um but uh beyond that, you know, um I mean in term,s of that particular issue, I would say if you have been gambling, and you're repentant of it, one of the things you would need to do in your repentance of that and giving it up would be to give away any winnings you had to people who are in. need, you know"],[2553.97,"Um but yeah, so that's that that that kind of money."],[2559.83,"And and um is not is not a good thing in the sense of good for us and for our Christian life."],[2569.99,"Um and so again, that kind of checks our you know, capitalist instincts of this this is you know the good life."],[2582.01,"And and you know, uh I've I've said before, not just because it's catchy, that Christianity isn't about living the good life, it's about living a good life."],[2591.37,"And those can be two entirely opposite things."],[2605.41,"But there was a parishioner in some church that he was in who had a bar and strip joint kind of business and who gave a big chunk of money to the church."],[2622.77,"And the church had these had problems with that accepting the for obvious reasons."],[2629.97,"Yeah. And um so he I think the bishop came was brought in on this."],[2638.37,"And the bishop said, Well, you know here's this money do you want it to go to god or do you want it to go to satan uh so that meant accept the money uh and uh the because otherwise you know he's gonna spend it on like who knows what yeah who knows what yeah expanding his business i i never i never was entirely comfortable with that and at the same time didn't have a good answer for it, so what what's your thoughts on that?"],[2674.05,"Well, to me, I'm less concerned about money than i am about people."],[2683.01,"So my concern would go to that person who owns that business."],[2685.85,"s that And for the sake of his soul, he need to sell business."],[2691.25,"Yeah. And then if we get him to that point of repentance, where he's sold the business."],[2698.71,"And then you say, Well, he just made all this money selling that business."],[2702.95,"What do we do with it?"],[2704.47,"My answer is we need to give it to people who are in need."],[2708.23,"Through the church or otherwise."],[2710.69,"Then yes, then you use the money for good."],[2713.97,"But to me, just the question of he wrote this check, what do we do with the check leaves out that factor of well, what do we do about him?"],[2723.89,"Uh-huh and the state of his church."],[2727.53,"The state of his soul."],[2729.09,"Sure And I would say it's better for him to be coming to the church than not."],[2735.77,"But when he's coming to the church, he needs to be called to repentance."],[2740.41,"And we tend I'm speaking about religious conservatives now."],[2747.61,"The Orthodox Church, the whole Orthodox Church is religiously conservative."],[2751.45,"Obviously. And we tend to have a great clarity about this kind of thing with sexual sin."],[2758.51,"Where we say, well, no, you can't come to church and call yourself a Christian and be living this or that lifestyle."],[2763.89,"Be living with someone out of wedlock or be doing this or that or the other."],[2768.07,"Or homosexuality or whatever you want to say."],[2770.16,"Any of these lifestyle things."],[2772.16,"And we have a great lack of clarity, at least in the United States, about sins having to do with economics and that kind of thing."],[2784.14,"And the same thing applies to someone who's involved in sinful business dealings that applies to someone who's in a sinful sexual relationship, for example."],[2794.03,"You can't just keep doing this."],[2796.77,"There has to be a point where you say, no, this is wrong."],[2799.57,"I'm going to repent and I'm going to stop."],[2801.57,"In all those cases."],[2803.39,"All those cases."],[2805.03,"Um so yeah, but I I think we need more clarity on both sides."],[2810.91,"On both sides."],[2811.95,"Because you go to you go to, for example, a Protestant denomination that's religiously liberal and theologically liberal, and it's the exact reverse."],[2819.52,"They'll tell you how evil Wall Street bankers are all day and all night."],[2823.68,"But any kind of sexual sin they just wink at."],[2827.36,"You know. So it's the reverse."],[2830.24,"And the the truth is that there are all kinds of sin into which we humans fall, you know, and we are not inclined of ourselves very often to repent of it, you know, and change our lives."],[2844.24,"And so part of what has to happen when people come to church is that every single one of us needs to be called on again and again to look at our lives and to repent of the things that are wrong and change them."],[2856.48,"You know, whether it's economic or sexual or something else."],[2861.24,"Entirely gluttony, you know, whatever it might be, you know, um violence and anger, you know, anything like that."],[2871.72,"We can't just say, well, well, it's good you're coming to church."],[2875.16,"I mean, it is good that you're coming to church, but there's another piece here that has to, you know, you also need to stop, you know, you know, if you're beating your spouse on Saturday night and then coming to church Sunday morning, we can't just say, Well, I'm glad at least he's coming to church."],[2895.16,"We have to say new."],[2896.68,"Or if you're committing adultery, uh, you know, you're cheating on your husband."],[2900.12,"We can't just say, Well, it's good that she's coming to church at least, you know. it's gotta be a but it seems like a way of doing that though is not accepting those kind of contributions"],[2911.68,"It seems like kind of a passive way of of saying you're welcome to come to church, and we're glad you're making that step of coming to church."],[2920.24,"Yeah, but uh we're not going to accept this contribution because what you're doing is against the when you sell the business, when you repent of it."],[2929.84,"But yeah, we're not gonna take 10% of the proceeds from a strip club, a strip club every month."],[2940.0,"Yeah, we're disaster."],[2941.44,"We're not we're not gonna do that."],[2942.72,"But what puzzles me in the background of that story is what goes on all the time with this this guy. in the church who owns a strip joint"],[2952.28,"Do people ever say, Why do you own a strip joint?"],[2955.64,"Or you know, you know you you own a strip joint."],[2959.56,"Do you think that's right?"],[2960.6,"Or you know everybody seems to just miss the situation."],[2966.2,"So see, I'm I'm an outlaw."],[2968.6,"And so what I would do in that situation, honestly, is uh I would show up at his place of business unexpectedly and tell him I was gonna bless it and ask him to line everybody up so we could pray."],[2985.88,"And that might solve the problem one way or the other."],[2991.56,"Might very well never see him again, but you know."],[2994.92,"If he stays after that, that's the news"],[2995.92,"Um but yes."],[2999.84,"Yeah, well, yeah, then you'll hopefully change his view of some things."],[3012.56,"Yeah, there's there's there's a famous story of I'm trying to remember which saint it was."],[3017.68,"There's a priest in Greece who later became a saint."],[3020.32,"And in Greece, when they go to bless houses after Theophany, you know, it's everybody."],[3024.96,"So they just kind of go down the road."],[3027.2,"You know. And he went into this place that was a brothel."],[3032.16,"And he just went in and started blessing it."],[3034.48,"And and asked the women working there to come and kiss the cross and prayed with them and everything."],[3039.76,"And uh when he first went in, the madam who was running it came up to him and said, What you know, what are you doing here, Father?"],[3047.32,"You know, you can't don't you know what this place is?"],[3049.88,"He said, I know exactly what this place is, and that's why I came here."],[3053.64,"You know, so there is a history of that in the Orthodox Church."],[3059.16,"But the the core here is and and that this relates to here in Corinth is that uh our economic life is not lawless."],[3070.92,"It's not sort of outside of God's law, it's not sort of outside of the realm of sin, you know."],[3075.84,"You know, and the the emphasis for Saint Paul in particular, and that we see reflected here by St."],[3082.28,"Luke in Corinth, is that is that we're to. you know support ourselves and our families by working by the work of our hands"],[3092.16,"And not allow our not pursue, not chase after, and not allow ourselves to become sort of comfortable and lax and well fed and these kind of things."],[3101.36,"And the the the fasting and these disciplines of the church help us to do that."],[3105.52,"And giving alms and tithing to the church."],[3108.96,"Help us to do that."],[3110.16,"In a modern, in a modern economic model where it's like capitalism or socialism, which it doesn't fit neatly in either."],[3118.06,"Because the government is completely uninvolved in this."],[3120.7,"In what I'm saying right now."],[3122.06,"I'm not saying anything about what the government should do."],[3125.1,"I'm talking about what we as individual Christians, what we should do."],[3128.78,"Listen next time. as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God"],[3136.02,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[3141.3,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":22.24,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: Paul's second missionary journey","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at the start of <b>Acts 18</b>; first Acts study not yet online - we're <b>43 episodes ahead</b> of release</li><li class=\"d0\">Second journey: Paul has <b>Silas and Timothy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">revisited churches planted with <b>Barnabas</b> in Asia Minor (Turkey)</li><li class=\"d1\">dream → <b>Macedonia</b> → now down into <b>Greece proper</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Last chapter: <b>Athens</b> - <b>Luke</b> with them now (switch from \"they\" to <b>\"we\"</b>)</li><li class=\"d0\">Athens = \"as Greek as you can get\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's pattern: <b>synagogue first</b>, preach Christ to Jews and the ring of interested Gentiles</li><li class=\"d1\">Gentiles who frequented and <b>endowed</b> the synagogue but wouldn't become Jews</li><li class=\"d1\">many converts from that second group → <b>strife</b>: Paul \"raiding\" members and <b>donors</b></li></ul>"},{"t":209.38,"lv":3,"h":"Athens recap: first sermon to pure pagans","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">In Athens Paul branched out beyond the synagogue</li><li class=\"d1\">other days of the week → the <b>marketplace</b>: news, ideas, philosophy, politics</li><li class=\"d1\">\"ready audience\"</li><li class=\"d0\">First time in Acts a sermon aimed <b>straight at pagans</b> - no Old Testament link, no Jewish community</li><li class=\"d0\">Method: go <b>way back</b> - <b>Tower of Babel</b>, before the nations were separated and before Abraham</li><li class=\"d1\">common heritage: <b>God created all of us</b></li><li class=\"d1\">since Babel you've followed things that \"aren't really gods\"</li><li class=\"d1\">the true God who made everything is <b>calling you back to himself</b></li></ul>"},{"t":306.34,"lv":2,"h":"Q: Is there a letter to the Athenians?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: no letter of Paul to the Athenians - would have a different angle</li><li class=\"d0\">Closest thing (debated): <b>Colossians</b></li><li class=\"d1\">debate whether it addresses <b>paganism</b> or a type of <b>Jewish mysticism</b> - probably a mix</li><li class=\"d1\">Colossae had <b>no strong Torah-based</b> Jewish background</li><li class=\"d1\">contrast <b>Galatians</b>: law and circumcision</li><li class=\"d1\">Colossians = more <b>cosmic issues</b>, cosmology</li><li class=\"d0\">No address to philosophers from Paul directly</li><li class=\"d0\">We get that from <b>St. Justin Martyr</b>, 2nd c. - Greek philosophy and Christianity</li><li class=\"d1\">\"a hundred years later\"</li></ul>"},{"t":418.98,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 18:1 - Paul departs Athens for Corinth","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>18:1</b>: after these things Paul left Athens, went to <b>Corinth</b></li><li class=\"d0\">One of the more famous cities: <b>1 and 2 Corinthians</b></li><li class=\"d0\">From Paul's own references: at least <b>four letters</b> to Corinth</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>2 Corinthians</b> = at least <b>two letters</b> put together</li><li class=\"d1\">in Greek you can \"see the seam\" - two complete letters joined</li><li class=\"d0\">Why: books <b>circulated together</b></li><li class=\"d1\">scrolls = set amount of space - \"if you pay the money, you fill the space\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>codex</b> = long sheet folded and bound, like modern pages - \"get your money's worth\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Minor prophets</b> = one scroll, the <b>Book of the Twelve</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Fathers cite them as <b>one book</b>; longest = four chapters</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's epistles collected and circulating together <b>by AD 100</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>General/Catholic epistles</b> (James, 1-2 Peter, 1-3 John, Jude) likewise short → copied as a unit</li></ul>"},{"t":602.0,"lv":2,"h":"Why so many letters to Corinth","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not only Paul: <b>1 Clement</b>, end of 1st c., in the <b>Apostolic Fathers</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>St. Clement</b>, bishop of Rome - 3rd after Peter (<b>Linus</b>, then Clement)</li><li class=\"d1\">Peter mentions him; knew Peter directly</li><li class=\"d1\">wrote on behalf of the whole church at Rome to Corinth</li><li class=\"d0\">Reason for all the letters: <b>not</b> that Corinth was important then</li><li class=\"d1\">it was in an earlier era</li><li class=\"d0\">Because of <b>horrible problems</b> in the church there that kept recurring</li><li class=\"d1\">\"really severe serious problems\" - \"a big mess\"</li></ul>"},{"t":688.12,"lv":2,"h":"Greek Corinth and Roman Corinth","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">This is the <b>second Corinth</b> - <b>Roman Corinth</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Original <b>Greek Corinth</b>: ancient, very important</li><li class=\"d1\">on the tiny <b>land bridge</b> between northern Greece and the <b>Peloponnesus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">all <b>trade roads</b> ran through it</li><li class=\"d1\">where city-states met → the <b>Corinthian League</b> = \"the NATO of Greece\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Trade + politics → <b>wealthy</b> → <b>decadent</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"Corinthianized\" = a Greek verb for <b>debauched</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"party like a Corinthian\" - cf. \"party like a rock star\"; \"wild New Orleans\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Mid-<b>2nd c. BC</b> Rome invades Greece</li><li class=\"d1\">Corinth setting up as defensive point → Rome <b>burned it to the ground</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"made an example\" → Peloponnesus decided not to resist</li><li class=\"d0\">Romans rebuilt it on the ruins as <b>display of Roman power</b></li></ul>"},{"t":841.61,"lv":3,"h":"Roman Corinth's reputation","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Roman Corinth: also wealthy trade city, similar reputation</li><li class=\"d0\">Example: a man having an affair with his <b>mother-in-law</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul's refrain: <b>now you're in Christ</b>, be different from the other people in Corinth</li><li class=\"d1\">\"we've left that way of living behind\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not just a moral issue: major <b>pagan religious center</b></li><li class=\"d1\">mystery/initiatory cult of <b>Demeter</b>, goddess of harvest</li><li class=\"d1\">huge temple, thoroughly <b>excavated</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: Eleusinian mysteries? A: no - a <b>particular Corinthian</b> cult, evolved from a traditional Demeter temple</li></ul>"},{"t":889.75,"lv":2,"h":"Banquet halls: sacrifices were meals","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Big part of the temple complex = huge <b>banquet halls</b></li><li class=\"d1\">excavations: bones of animals, mostly <b>pigs</b> - primary Greek sacrifice</li><li class=\"d1\">also <b>sea urchin</b> remains etc. - unsure if sacrificed or just on the table</li><li class=\"d0\">We forget: <b>sacrifices were meals</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Protestants: \"Eucharist isn't a sacrifice, it's a meal\" - <b>distinction made no sense</b> in the ancient world</li><li class=\"d1\">portion to the gods, rest eaten by offerers → <b>eating a meal with the gods</b></li><li class=\"d1\">binds community together <b>with its god</b>; social and political elements too</li><li class=\"d0\">Explains Paul's long treatment in 1-2 Corinthians</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>meat offered to idols</b></li><li class=\"d1\">what will someone think seeing you <b>eat in a pagan temple</b></li><li class=\"d1\">participating in these meals = <b>civic life</b> of Corinth</li><li class=\"d0\">Aside: sea urchins - a \"Roman thing\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1066.79,"lv":3,"h":"Burning of the bones","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Impious Greeks joked: sacrifice = <b>\"burning of the bones\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">kept all useful parts - <b>meat, leather</b></li><li class=\"d1\">burnt what they couldn't use: <b>bones, teeth, hooves</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Contrast <b>Leviticus</b>: God specifies parts to offer - all the <b>best parts</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"I want the best parts; you take the rest\"</li><li class=\"d1\">this piece to the <b>priests</b>, this to the <b>people</b>, these burnt</li><li class=\"d1\">burnt = <b>choice, prize portions</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Pagans did the <b>exact opposite</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1152.37,"lv":2,"h":"Civic life of Corinth; the Acropolis","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Point of all this: <b>idolatry, pagan religion, liberal sexuality</b> all on <b>public display</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"that's what it meant to be a Corinthian\"</li><li class=\"d1\">a church of converted Corinthians = <b>high maintenance</b> for Paul: continuously address, remind</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: <b>Acrocorinth</b> = the Acropolis of Corinth? A: yes - where many temple excavations are</li><li class=\"d0\"><i>Acro</i> = <b>high</b> (cf. acrobat)</li><li class=\"d1\">hill/mound with <b>temples</b> and <b>civic government buildings</b></li><li class=\"d1\">main city below; <b>docks</b> = commerce and \"seedier\" things</li><li class=\"d0\">Pagan religious logic: three Olympian brothers</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Zeus</b> = sky, the Acropolis, king, power</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Pluto/Hades</b> = run-of-the-mill city and marketplace - also <b>money</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Poseidon</b> = the docks and sea</li><li class=\"d0\">Scholars: <b>cosmic geography</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Bible has a little: <b>Zion</b>, Sinai, Tabor, <b>Hermon</b> - \"tallest of the mountains\" because that's where the temple was</li><li class=\"d1\">real reason: higher points <b>best defended</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1404.42,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 18:2 - Aquila and Priscilla","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul comes to Corinth: \"the thick of pagan Roman life\"</li><li class=\"d1\">no longer little towns near Palestine, synagogue, Jewish countrymen</li><li class=\"d1\">\"going to cause some issues\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>18:2</b>: finds a Jew <b>Aquila</b>, born in <b>Pontus</b>, recently from Italy with wife <b>Priscilla</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Claudius</b> commanded all Jews to leave <b>Rome</b></li><li class=\"d0\">One of <b>several expulsions</b> of Jews from Rome in the 1st c.</li><li class=\"d0\">Jews = <b>scapegoat</b> when things went wrong - before it was Christians</li></ul>"},{"t":1437.7,"lv":2,"h":"Roman quid pro quo and the Sibylline books","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Romans: <b>quid pro quo</b> with the gods</li><li class=\"d1\">we do the ritual right → gods give success in war</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Sibylline Oracles</b>: ancient cryptic books, \"Nostradamus-like\"</li><li class=\"d1\">stored under a <b>temple in Rome</b>; only a couple of priests could read</li><li class=\"d1\">consulted for any crisis - even a <b>comet</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Hannibal</b>: told to bury alive a Roman and a barbarian (Scythian?) in the <b>marketplace</b> - they did it</li><li class=\"d1\">\"that's how seriously they took it\" → now we beat Hannibal</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>5th c.</b>: Rome sacked → crowd: \"because we turned Christian,\" consult the books</li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>Christian governor</b> publicly <b>burned</b> them: \"no going back\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Later <b>counterfeits</b> (6th-7th c.) - medieval Christians cited them as prophesying Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">now known to be <b>bogus</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1667.98,"lv":3,"h":"Jews as scapegoats; Claudius, then Nero","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">If they did the rites and it still failed → <b>religious crisis</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Easy answer with a big Jewish community: <b>\"these people refused to sacrifice to our gods\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">gods angry → <b>scapegoat</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Claudius</b>: expelled them all from Rome</li><li class=\"d1\">so when the capital worshipped, it was <b>everybody</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Nero</b> = first emperor to use the same reasoning against <b>Christians</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"they won't sacrifice to our gods, so it's their fault\"</li><li class=\"d1\">source of much later <b>persecution</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Expelled Jews had to <b>relocate</b> - hence Aquila and Priscilla</li></ul>"},{"t":1761.54,"lv":2,"h":"Priscilla and Aquila: money and names","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul later lists <b>Priscilla and Aquila</b> - <b>wife first</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"pretty sure bet\" she was from the <b>family with the money</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Jews living in the capital needed <b>money and/or connections</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Both have <b>Latin names</b>: in Italy a long time, <b>acculturated</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why not Jerusalem? Probably <b>didn't know a lick of Aramaic</b>, firmly Greco-Roman</li><li class=\"d1\">out of Rome → another major cultural center: <b>Corinth, Alexandria, Athens</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1865.3,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 18:3 - Tentmaking","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>18:3</b>: because he was of the <b>same trade</b>, he stayed and worked with them</li><li class=\"d0\">Tents: \"we don't use a lot of tents anymore\" - camping, circuses, rentals</li><li class=\"d0\">Then: <b>Roman military</b> - maneuvers, travel; probably their main customer in Rome</li><li class=\"d1\">travelers on long trade routes <b>east</b>: <b>India, Iran, Afghanistan</b></li><li class=\"d1\">past a certain point \"you're doing some <b>camping, caravanning</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Important <b>industry</b>: tanning, <b>leather, canvas</b>, support structures</li></ul>"},{"t":1967.57,"lv":3,"h":"Paul's trade; honest work","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul knew and had worked this trade - <b>not abnormal</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"not like he went to seminary and had a <b>student loan</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Even <b>rabbis</b>: many worked a <b>trade</b> during the week</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul stresses <b>working with your hands</b> in his epistles</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. John Chrysostom</b>: suspicious of wealth made without making things</li><li class=\"d1\">wealth from <b>shifting money around</b>, deals - looks on it \"a little shamely\"</li><li class=\"d1\">comes from Paul: <b>honest work</b>, supporting yourself</li><li class=\"d0\">Asides: apart from <b>usury</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2063.27,"lv":2,"h":"Paul takes no money for the gospel","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Priscilla and Aquila: <b>tentmaking operation</b> moved with them to Corinth</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul works to <b>support himself and the mission</b></li><li class=\"d0\">His point in the epistles: <b>never took money in exchange for the gospel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">didn't want \"so who's gonna put me up and feed me?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">no appearance of <b>running a racket</b>; wanted to be <b>above reproach</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Did take <b>collections</b> - for the <b>church in Jerusalem</b></li><li class=\"d1\">mostly poor Palestinian Jewish <b>peasants</b></li><li class=\"d1\">large collection from wealthier new <b>Gentile churches</b> → carried back to the poorer</li><li class=\"d0\">Not a <b>private jet or big house</b> - \"this is not Jim and Tammy Faye\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2170.93,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 18:4 - Sabbath preaching; 'he who does not work'","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>18:4</b>: reasoned in the synagogue <b>every Sabbath</b>, persuaded Jews and Greeks</li><li class=\"d0\">Six days work; <b>Sabbath</b> synagogue; <b>Lord's Day</b> = Christian meeting with <b>Eucharist</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Digression: <b>pet peeve</b> - misquoted verse</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>he who does not work shall not eat</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">in a letter to Corinth (his words)</li><li class=\"d0\">You'd think it's about working with your hands</li></ul>"},{"t":2235.33,"lv":2,"h":"The verse in context: the agape meal","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">In context Paul is talking about the <b>Eucharist</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Christians still at the <b>synagogue on the Sabbath</b> - scriptures and preaching = first part of liturgy</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Sunday</b> Eucharist = second part</li><li class=\"d0\">Eucharist within an <b>agape feast / love meal</b></li><li class=\"d1\">full meal, like Christ's with his disciples, Eucharist at the end</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem: some arrive <b>early and eat all the food</b></li><li class=\"d1\">latecomers get <b>nothing</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Early ones = <b>wealthy</b>, don't work on the first day of the week</li><li class=\"d1\">later ones = those who <b>had to work</b>, come after a day's labor</li><li class=\"d0\">So the verse means the <b>exact opposite</b> of how it's usually used</li></ul>"},{"t":2357.54,"lv":3,"h":"Argument with the Episcopal bishop; asceticism","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Story: <b>Episcopal Bishop of New Orleans</b> quoted the verse <b>against welfare</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he wrote objecting - \"liberal me\" - bishop politely brushed him off</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's point: some in the Empire <b>did well by working</b> (Priscilla and Aquila)</li><li class=\"d1\">others <b>inherited</b> money and lived in leisure</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's answer to them: <b>give up wealth, live by the work of your hands</b></li><li class=\"d1\">this emphasis → <b>monasticism</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>St. Anthony the Great</b>: inherited wealth, gave it all away, supported himself</li><li class=\"d1\">origin of <b>monastic poverty</b>: being <b>idly wealthy</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2459.06,"lv":3,"h":"American goal vs Christian goal","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">American culture: work a while, then <b>coast and be comfortable</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Christianity: <b>not</b> the goal to be totally comfortable and indulge myself</li><li class=\"d1\">if I get there, <b>start getting rid of stuff</b></li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>asceticism</b>; keep working - not good to be <b>comfortable, sated, well fed</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: <b>lottery winnings</b> and tithing - denominations split over it</li><li class=\"d1\">A: shouldn't be gambling; if <b>repentant</b>, give winnings to <b>people in need</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Checks our <b>capitalist instincts</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"Christianity isn't about living <b>the good life</b>, it's about living <b>a good life</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"two entirely opposite things\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2605.41,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: Donations from a strip club owner","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: parishioner owning a <b>bar and strip joint</b> gave a big sum</li><li class=\"d1\">bishop: \"do you want it to go to God or to Satan?\" - accept it</li><li class=\"d1\">\"never entirely comfortable... didn't have a good answer\"</li><li class=\"d0\">A: <b>less concerned about money than about people</b></li><li class=\"d1\">concern = <b>his soul</b>: he needs to <b>sell the business</b></li><li class=\"d1\">if he repents and sells → give proceeds to <b>people in need</b>, church or otherwise</li><li class=\"d1\">\"what do we do with the check\" leaves out <b>what do we do about him</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Better he's coming to church - but he must be <b>called to repentance</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: simply refusing contributions? A: yes - <b>won't take 10% of strip club proceeds</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2747.61,"lv":3,"h":"Clarity on sin: sexual and economic","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Religious conservatives, <b>Orthodox</b> included: clear on <b>sexual sin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">can't call yourself Christian and live with someone out of wedlock, etc.</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>great lack of clarity</b> in the US about <b>economic</b> sins</li><li class=\"d0\">Same for sinful business as sinful relationship: <b>repent and stop</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Reverse in <b>theologically liberal Protestant</b> bodies</li><li class=\"d1\">Wall Street bankers evil all day; sexual sin - <b>they wink at it</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Every one of us must be <b>called on again and again</b> to repent</li><li class=\"d1\">economic, sexual, <b>gluttony, violence, anger</b></li><li class=\"d1\">beating your spouse Saturday, church Sunday - <b>can't just say \"glad he's coming\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same for adultery</li></ul>"},{"t":2966.2,"lv":3,"h":"Blessing the business; the Greek priest","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">He: \"I'm an <b>outlaw</b>\" - would show up at the business, say he's going to <b>bless it</b>, line everybody up to <b>pray</b></li><li class=\"d1\">might never see him again; if he stays, \"that's the news\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Story: a <b>Greek priest</b> (later a saint) blessing houses after <b>Theophany</b></li><li class=\"d1\">went into a <b>brothel</b>, had the women <b>kiss the cross</b>, prayed with them</li><li class=\"d1\">madam: \"don't you know what this place is?\" - \"<b>I know exactly what it is, that's why I came</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"history of that in the Orthodox Church\"</li></ul>"},{"t":3059.16,"lv":3,"h":"Economic life is not lawless","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Link to Corinth: our <b>economic life is not lawless</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not outside God's law or <b>the realm of sin</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul (and Luke here): <b>support ourselves and our families by the work of our hands</b></li><li class=\"d1\">don't chase after or become <b>comfortable, lax, well fed</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Helps: <b>fasting</b>, church disciplines, <b>giving alms</b>, <b>tithing</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Fits neither <b>capitalism nor socialism</b></li><li class=\"d1\">government not involved here; not saying what government should do</li><li class=\"d1\">talking about what <b>we as individual Christians</b> should do</li></ul>"}]});
